The following key stakeholders will be invited to participate in a targeted consultation:
•	EU Member States (as coastal States, port States and flag States) public authorities (ministries responsible for maritime transport and marine pollution, agencies and prosecutors/judges);
•	Shipping operators/companies and industry associations (such as masters, owners, operators, charterers and cargo owners);
•	Third (non-EU) states whose ships call in EU ports;
•	Recognised organisations/ classification societies;
•	Relevant international, regional and European bodies and agencies dealing with marine pollution from ships: such as the International Maritime Organisation (IMO), the International Criminal Police Organisation INTERPOL (ICPO-INTERPOL), the European Maritime Safety Agency (EMSA), the Helsinki Convention HELCOM, the Barcelona Convention/MENELAS, the Bonn Agreement, the Copenhagen Agreement, the Lisbon Agreement,/, OSPAR, and the Black Sea Commission.
•	Professional network of prosecutors: such as the North Sea Network of Investigators and Prosecutors (NSN) and the Network of Prosecutors on Environmental Crime in the Baltic Sea Region (ENPRO).
•	Environmental associations and organisations.
•	The Protection and Indemnity (P&I) Clubs and the Marine Insurers.
Stakeholders who will be invited to the targeted consultation may also participate in this survey.

All citizens and organisations are welcome to contribute to this consultation. The following parties might have a specific interest in doing so: 
•	ITS service providers;
•	Road operators;
•	Transport operators;
•	Vehicle and equipment manufacturers/ suppliers;
•	national, regional and local administrations;
•	organisations representing social partners, civil society, consumers and passengers
•	NGOs dealing in particular with mobility, digitalisation and the environment; 
•	research and academia; education & training institutions and organisations.

The consultation is primarily addressed to the following stakeholder groups: 
- Public authorities at European, national, regional level, notably those responsible for regulating/supporting air transportation and those responsible for regulating/supporting the production and uptake of sustainable aviation fuels;- European and international organisations dealing with aviation and transport fuel issues including non-governmental organisations, especially in the area of environmental policy;
- Actors of the jet fuel production industry, including the sustainable aviation fuel production industry, i.e. feedstock producers, feedstock wholesalers, fuel producers, fuel retailers, fuel suppliers, sustainability certifiers and others; - Actors of the air transport industry, including airlines, airports, aircraft manufacturers, air navigation service providers; -The general public.

All citizens and organisations are welcome to contribute to this consultation.

The main stakeholder groups that may be interested in this initiative include: 

•	EEA Member States plus UK (administrations and inspectorates) in their capacity as flag State and coastal State;
•	Social partners in sea fisheries at EU and national level;
•	EU fisheries advisory councils;
•	Classification societies; 
•	Fishers’ associations;
•	Shipbuilders and equipment manufacturers;
•	Academics and Research institutes

consultationObjective:
The aim of the envisaged consultations is to seek information and feedback from the most relevant stakeholders and from the wider public in relation to the main directions of the planned EU Urban Mobility framework. More specifically, the consultation should provide feedback as to how the EU urban areas can:
•	contribute to reducing transport greenhouse emissions and to other objectives of the Sustainable and Smart Mobility Strategy including its milestone to have 100 European cities carbon neutral by 2030; 
•	improve the quality of life of the EU urban population by addressing urban mobility challenges such as air pollution, congestion, noise, accessibility, road safety while promoting physical activity;
•	increase the support to, and use of, the most sustainable transport modes (in particular public transport and active mobility) as well as zero-emission urban logistics.

targetGroups:
